3. Restricted Monetization requires a separate paid license

The free permissions in section 2 do not authorize:

(a) selling, licensing for a fee, renting, or otherwise distributing or
providing the Software or a Modified Version in exchange for money or other
commercial consideration, whether standalone or bundled with another product;

(b) providing a paid hosted, managed, software-as-a-service, API, or similar
service that makes the Software's code-advice or code-analysis functionality
available to customers or other third parties, including when access is part
of a paid subscription or bundle.

Calling a charge an installation, customization, access, or other service fee
does not avoid this restriction if, in substance, the transaction supplies a
paid copy of the Software or a Modified Version, or a paid service in (b).

Paying employees or contractors to operate an internal deployment does not,
by itself, constitute Restricted Monetization. Independent support or training
fees are permitted when they do not supply a paid copy, paid hosted access, or
other transaction described above. Voluntary donations that do not condition
access to the Software or its hosted functionality are permitted.

Before undertaking Restricted Monetization, you must obtain a separate
written commercial license from the Licensor or its authorized representative
and comply with its agreed payment terms. No price, royalty percentage,
payment schedule, or commercial permission is implied by this public license.
Contact the publisher of the repository from which this release was obtained
to request a commercial agreement with the authorized rights holder.

